Here are the SQL schemas and the LogDeltaEvent.sh helper script supporting both MySQL/MariaDB and PostgreSQL, designed to read your cloud-init database credentials and integrate with Wintersys ADT.

1. Database Table Schemas

Run the appropriate schema once during your database provisioning or end-of-build seeding step.

MySQL / MariaDB Schema

sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS adt_delta_events (
    id BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    event_type VARCHAR(16) NOT NULL,
    object_key VARCHAR(512) NOT NULL,
    source_ip VARCHAR(45) NOT NULL,
    created_at BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,
    INDEX idx_created_at (created_at)
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4;
Use code with caution.

PostgreSQL Schema

sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS adt_delta_events (
    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    event_type VARCHAR(16) NOT NULL,
    object_key VARCHAR(512) NOT NULL,
    source_ip VARCHAR(45) NOT NULL,
    created_at BIGINT NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_adt_delta_created_at ON adt_delta_events (created_at);
Use code with caution.

2. LogDeltaEvent.sh Helper Script

Save this script to ${HOME}/utilities/database/LogDeltaEvent.sh and make it executable (chmod +x). It loads your local cloud-init database credentials file, escapes single quotes to prevent SQL syntax issues, and inserts the delta event row.
bash
#!/bin/sh
#####################################################################################
# Description: Logs a filesystem delta archive or deletion log event into MySQL/MariaDB
# or PostgreSQL using local cloud-init database credentials.
# Usage: LogDeltaEvent.sh <event_type> <object_key> <source_ip>
#####################################################################################

event_type="${1}"
object_key="${2}"
source_ip="${3}"

if [ -z "${event_type}" ] || [ -z "${object_key}" ] || [ -z "${source_ip}" ]; then
    exit 1
fi

# Load credentials injected via cloud-init (adjust path if using database_descriptor.dat)
CREDS_FILE="/root/.adt_db_creds"
if [ -f "${CREDS_FILE}" ]; then
    . "${CREDS_FILE}"
else
    exit 1
fi

# Expected variables from CREDS_FILE:
# DB_ENGINE ("mysql", "mariadb", or "postgres")
# DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASS

created_at="`/usr/bin/date +%s`"

# Escape any single quotes in paths
safe_type="`/bin/echo "${event_type}" | /bin/sed "s/'/''/g"`"
safe_key="`/bin/echo "${object_key}" | /bin/sed "s/'/''/g"`"
safe_ip="`/bin/echo "${source_ip}" | /bin/sed "s/'/''/g"`"

sql_query="INSERT INTO adt_delta_events (event_type, object_key, source_ip, created_at) VALUES ('${safe_type}', '${safe_key}', '${safe_ip}', ${created_at});"

case "${DB_ENGINE}" in
    mysql|mariadb)
        MYSQL_PWD="${DB_PASS}" /usr/bin/mysql \
            -h "${DB_HOST}" \
            -P "${DB_PORT:-3306}" \
            -u "${DB_USER}" \
            "${DB_NAME}" \
            -e "${sql_query}"
        ;;
    postgres|postgresql)
        PGPASSWORD="${DB_PASS}" /usr/bin/psql \
            -h "${DB_HOST}" \
            -p "${DB_PORT:-5432}" \
            -U "${DB_USER}" \
            -d "${DB_NAME}" \
            -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
            -q -c "${sql_query}"
        ;;
    *)
        exit 1
        ;;
esac
Use code with caution.

How It Fits Together

• Zero S3 Polling: Whenever a node uploads an additions tarball or deletions log via PutToDatastore.sh, it immediately writes a lightweight row containing the exact S3 object_key and source_ip.
• Self-Filtering on Peer Nodes: Peer webservers store their last processed event ID in /var/run/adt_last_delta_id and run SELECT id, event_type, object_key FROM adt_delta_events WHERE id > $LAST_ID AND source_ip != '$MY_IP' ORDER BY id ASC. This prevents nodes from re-downloading their own uploads while fetching every peer delta in exact chronological order.
• Easy Table Housekeeping: Because created_at is indexed as a Unix epoch integer, you can run a simple daily cleanup query (DELETE FROM adt_delta_events WHERE created_at < UNIX_TIMESTAMP() - 86400;) so the table stays tiny in memory.
Would you like me to write the incoming peer-node consumer script that queries adt_delta_events, downloads the listed S3 archive, and extracts or deletes the files locally?


Here is the incoming peer-node consumer script (ProcessIncomingDeltas.sh). It queries adt_delta_events for any new records created by other servers, downloads the exact S3 archive or deletion log via ADT's GetFromDatastore.sh, applies the changes locally to both ${target_directory} and ${target_directory}1, and advances a local high-water mark ID.

ProcessIncomingDeltas.sh (Incoming Peer-Node Consumer)

Save this script to ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/ProcessIncomingDeltas.sh (or alongside your existing incoming sync scripts) and make it executable (chmod +x).
bash
#!/bin/sh
#####################################################################################
# Description: Polls adt_delta_events in MySQL/MariaDB or PostgreSQL for new delta
# archives/deletion logs from peer servers, downloads them via GetFromDatastore.sh,
# and applies them locally to both the live webroot and shadow directory.
# Usage: ProcessIncomingDeltas.sh <target_directory> <bucket_type>
#####################################################################################

target_directory="${1}"
bucket_type="${2}"

if [ -z "${target_directory}" ] || [ -z "${bucket_type}" ]; then
    exit 1
fi

# Prevent overlapping executions on the consumer side
lock_file="/var/lock/adt_incoming_${bucket_type}.lock"
exec 8>"${lock_file}"
if ! /usr/bin/flock -n 8; then
    exit 0
fi

# Load credentials injected via cloud-init
CREDS_FILE="/root/.adt_db_creds"
if [ -f "${CREDS_FILE}" ]; then
    . "${CREDS_FILE}"
else
    exit 1
fi

machine_ip="`${HOME}/utilities/processing/GetIP.sh`"
state_dir="${HOME}/runtime/filesystem_sync/${bucket_type}/incoming"
/bin/mkdir -p "${state_dir}/additions" "${state_dir}/deletions"

last_id_file="${state_dir}/last_delta_id.dat"
if [ -f "${last_id_file}" ]; then
    last_id="`/bin/cat "${last_id_file}"`"
else
    last_id="0"
fi

# Query only events newer than last_id and NOT originated by this machine
sql_query="SELECT id, event_type, object_key FROM adt_delta_events WHERE id > ${last_id} AND source_ip != '${machine_ip}' ORDER BY id ASC;"

events=""
case "${DB_ENGINE}" in
    mysql|mariadb)
        events="`MYSQL_PWD="${DB_PASS}" /usr/bin/mysql \
            -h "${DB_HOST}" \
            -P "${DB_PORT:-3306}" \
            -u "${DB_USER}" \
            -N -B \
            "${DB_NAME}" \
            -e "${sql_query}"`"
        ;;
    postgres|postgresql)
        events="`PGPASSWORD="${DB_PASS}" /usr/bin/psql \
            -h "${DB_HOST}" \
            -p "${DB_PORT:-5432}" \
            -U "${DB_USER}" \
            -d "${DB_NAME}" \
            -t -A -F '	' \
            -c "${sql_query}"`"
        ;;
    *)
        exit 1
        ;;
esac

# Exit cleanly if there are no new deltas
if [ -z "${events}" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

# Process each event sequentially in chronological order
/bin/echo "${events}" | while IFS='	' read -r event_id event_type object_key; do
    if [ -z "${event_id}" ] || [ -z "${object_key}" ]; then
        continue
    fi

    filename="`/usr/bin/basename "${object_key}"`"

    if [ "${event_type}" = "additions" ]; then
        local_tar="${state_dir}/additions/${filename}"
        ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/GetFromDatastore.sh "${bucket_type}" "${object_key}" "${local_tar}"

        if [ -f "${local_tar}" ]; then
            # Extract directly to absolute paths (populates both target_directory and target_directory1)
            /usr/bin/tar -xzpf "${local_tar}" -C / --same-owner --same-permissions
            /bin/rm -f "${local_tar}"
        fi

    elif [ "${event_type}" = "deletions" ]; then
        local_log="${state_dir}/deletions/${filename}"
        ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/GetFromDatastore.sh "${bucket_type}" "${object_key}" "${local_log}"

        if [ -f "${local_log}" ]; then
            while IFS= read -r del_path; do
                if [ -n "${del_path}" ] && [ -f "${del_path}" ]; then
                    /bin/rm -f "${del_path}"
                fi
            done < "${local_log}"
            /bin/rm -f "${local_log}"
        fi
    fi

    # Update high-water mark after each successful event
    /bin/echo "${event_id}" > "${last_id_file}"
done

# Clean up any empty directories left behind by deletions
/usr/bin/find "${target_directory}" -mindepth 1 -type d -empty -delete 2>/dev/null
/usr/bin/find "${target_directory}1" -mindepth 1 -type d -empty -delete 2>/dev/null
Use code with caution.

How It Integrates Safely with Your Outgoing Script

• Prevents Sync Loops (target_directory1 Alignment): Because the outgoing script packs both ${target_directory}/${rel_path} and ${target_directory}1/${rel_path} into the archive (and logs both in deletions), extracting to / updates the live webroot and the peer's local shadow directory simultaneously. When the peer's own outgoing rsync pass runs next, it sees zero differences and never re-broadcasts the same delta.
• Bootstrapping New Autoscalers: When a brand-new webserver spins up during an autoscale event and pulls the baseline snapshot/webroot, have your boot script query SELECT COALESCE(MAX(id), 0) FROM adt_delta_events; and write that number into last_delta_id.dat so the new node only processes deltas generated after it came online.
• Tab-Delimited Parsing (IFS=$'\t'): Both mysql -N -B and psql -t -A -F '\t' output clean tab-separated columns without borders or headers, ensuring object_key values are parsed reliably even if paths contain spaces.
