Yes, this is now a lean, production-grade architecture. By pairing cloud-init bootstrapping, database-backed configuration and delta signaling, and single-pass rsync delta archiving, you have eliminated all the major bottlenecks in the original setup.

Summary of What Makes This Setup Efficient

• Zero S3 Polling Overhead: Webservers check a single indexed integer query (WHERE id > last_id) in MySQL or PostgreSQL rather than issuing expensive S3 LIST requests across buckets.
• Minimal Disk & CPU Footprint: Replacing three rsync --checksum traversals with a single metadata-only rsync -rni --delete pass cuts disk read I/O dramatically, while flock prevents overlapping runs from stacking up.
• Clean Boot Sequence: Keeping SSL certificates in S3 and injecting database credentials via cloud-init lets webservers boot cleanly without circular dependencies, while deferring application config seeding to the end of the build keeps deployment logic simple.
• Echo-Free Peer Sync: Updating both ${target_directory} and ${target_directory}1 atomically on incoming extraction prevents peer webservers from re-broadcasting received deltas.

Three Minor Operational Safeguards to Keep in Mind

Even with a clean design, three small details will keep it reliable over months of autoscaling:
• Prevent Race Conditions Between Outgoing and Incoming Locks: Because both scripts modify ${target_directory} and ${target_directory}1, use a shared lock file (/var/lock/adt_sync_${bucket_type}.lock) in both scripts so a peer extraction never runs mid-way through a local rsync scan.
• Bootstrap New Autoscalers Safely: Ensure newly autoscaled nodes capture MAX(id) from adt_delta_events before they pull their baseline webroot archive, rather than after. Capturing MAX(id) first guarantees that any delta created during the 30–60 seconds it takes a new node to boot is still replayed and never missed.
• Automate Table Pruning: Add a lightweight daily cron job on one node (or via a database scheduled event) to purge rows older than 24–48 hours (DELETE FROM adt_delta_events WHERE created_at < ...) so the table remains tiny in memory.
